      "Geostrophic wind is the theoretical horizontal wind that blows parallel to isobars above the friction layer (roughly 1-2 km), resulting from an exact balance between the Pressure Gradient Force (PGF) and the Coriolis Force (CoF).\n\nBarometric slope (the rate of pressure change with horizontal distance, shown by isobar spacing) generates the PGF, which pushes air from high to low pressure perpendicular to isobars. As the parcel begins moving, the Coriolis force -- proportional to wind speed and latitude, acting at right angles to motion (right in the Northern Hemisphere, left in the Southern) -- progressively deflects it. Equilibrium is reached when CoF equals and opposes PGF, and the resultant wind flows parallel to the isobars rather than across them.\n\nSteeper barometric slope (closely packed isobars) produces stronger PGF and hence higher geostrophic wind speed; gently sloping pressure fields (widely spaced isobars) produce weaker winds. This relationship (V = PGF / (2 * omega * sin(phi) * rho)) underlies synoptic weather forecasting: it explains why winds are strong around deep depressions and near-calm in weak pressure fields, and why the geostrophic approximation weakens near the equator, where the Coriolis term approaches zero.",
