    section: "B",
    questionNumber: "5(b)",
    marks: 10,
    wordLimit: 150,
    questionText: "Discuss critically food and nutrition problems associated with the developing world.",
    modelAnswer:
      "The developing world faces a 'triple burden' of malnutrition: undernutrition (insufficient caloric/protein intake, chronic in Sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia), micronutrient deficiency ('hidden hunger' -- iron, vitamin A, iodine deficiencies affecting cognitive and physical development, especially in children and women), and a rapidly rising burden of overnutrition/obesity linked to nutrition transition toward processed, calorie-dense diets in urbanising regions.\n\nUnderlying causes include poverty and unequal food access rather than absolute food scarcity (Amartya Sen's entitlement approach), inadequate agricultural productivity and post-harvest losses, poor infrastructure and storage, conflict and climate-related crop failures, and inadequate social safety nets. Gender inequity compounds the problem -- women and girls often eat last and least within households, driving intergenerational malnutrition cycles (low birth weight, stunting).\n\nCritically, food security interventions have often emphasised calorie sufficiency (Green Revolution-style productivity gains) while under-addressing dietary diversity and micronutrient access, and climate change is now intensifying the crisis through erratic monsoons and heat stress on staple crops, making the problem structurally worse rather than resolved despite decades of aid and policy attention.",
    examinerNotes:
      "'Critically' requires more than description -- candidates should flag the entitlement/access dimension (Amartya Sen) versus mere food-availability framing, and note the coexistence of undernutrition with rising obesity (double/triple burden) as the modern complexity. Weak answers only list hunger statistics without critical analysis of causes or policy gaps.",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_B("5(b)"),
